---
import BaseLayout from "../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import ActionLink from "../../../components/content/ActionLink.astro";
import ContentGrid from "../../../components/structure/ContentGrid.astro";
import ContentSection from "../../../components/structure/ContentSection.astro";
import ExhibitGroup from "../../../components/content/ExhibitGroup.astro";
import ExhibitItem from "../../../components/content/ExhibitItem.astro";
import PageBody from "../../../components/structure/PageBody.astro";
import PageHeading from "../../../components/structure/PageHeading.astro";
import SiteHeader from "../../../components/structure/SiteHeader.astro";
import panelImage from "../../../../images/banners/cv_sarah_panel_ogt.jpg";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Seattle Exhibits | Connections Museum"
  description="Explore the working switches, telephone equipment, and outside plant at Connections Museum Seattle."
>
  <SiteHeader />
  <PageBody>
    <PageHeading level="h1">Seattle exhibits</PageHeading>
    <p><ActionLink href="/seattle.html">Plan your visit to the Seattle museum</ActionLink></p>

    <ContentSection id="exhibits" title="A working museum" headingLevel="h2">
      <ContentGrid columns={2}>
        <r-cell>
          <p>
            The museum sits inside a working telephone company central office in
            Georgetown. Once, rooms like this connected whole neighborhoods. Here,
            volunteers keep a century of switching equipment running so you can
            hear a call move through the machinery, not just look at a cabinet.
          </p>
        </r-cell>
        <r-cell>
          <p>
            Formerly the Herbert H. Warrick Jr. Museum of Communications, we're
            part of the Telecommunications History Group. Telephone Pioneer
            volunteers began building the museum in 1986, moving and reconnecting
            huge switch frames by hand. Their work continues as new volunteers
            learn to care for the machines. Take a tour or explore the public areas
            at your own pace; our guides are glad to talk through what you're seeing.
          </p>
        </r-cell>
      </ContentGrid>

      <p>
        Things don't stay frozen here. The big switches anchor the rooms, while
        smaller displays move as volunteers make space, restore equipment, and add
        new functions. Ask a guide what's changed since your last visit.
      </p>

      <ExhibitGroup
        id="central-office-switches"
        title="Working switches"
        headingLevel="h3"
        columns={3}
      >
        <r-cell>
          <ExhibitItem
            title="Panel switch"
            headingLevel="h4"
            image={panelImage}
            alt="Volunteer Sarah Autumn beside the museum's panel switching equipment"
            imageCaption="Sarah Autumn at the panel switch."
            imageSizes="(max-width: 600px) 100vw, 33vw"
            href="https://wiki.connections.museum/w/CMS:Panel_Switch"
            linkLabel="Explore the panel switch's history and workings"
          >
            <p>
              Seattle's 1923 panel switch still connects calls. A part called a
              sender takes in the number you dial; metal brushes then move across
              banks of contacts to find a path to the other phone. Watch the rods
              travel and listen to the work they do.
            </p>
          </ExhibitItem>
        </r-cell>
        <r-cell>
          <ExhibitItem title="Crossbar" headingLevel="h4">
            <p>
              The working No. 1 Crossbar belongs to the next chapter of automatic
              switching. Relays and moving contacts do the job of setting up a
              call. Volunteers maintain this machine alongside the panel switch,
              keeping both systems ready to demonstrate.
            </p>
          </ExhibitItem>
        </r-cell>
        <r-cell>
          <ExhibitItem title="Step-by-step and PBXs" headingLevel="h4">
            <p>
              You can also see working step-by-step and crossbar PBX equipment. A
              PBX is a switch for a building or organization: it connects phones
              inside the place and links them to the wider network. Ask a guide to
              show you how these smaller systems differ from the central-office
              machines.
            </p>
          </ExhibitItem>
        </r-cell>
      </ExhibitGroup>

      <ExhibitGroup
        id="power-and-ringing"
        title="Keeping the switches running"
        headingLevel="h3"
        columns={2}
      >
        <r-cell>
          <ExhibitItem
            title="Power board"
            headingLevel="h4"
            href="https://wiki.connections.museum/w/CMS:Power_Board"
            linkLabel="Read the power board's equipment notes"
          >
            <p>
              The switches need a steady supply of electricity. The power board
              distributes 48-volt office battery power to the museum's equipment,
              with meters and fuses that let volunteers watch what the machinery
              is drawing. This board came from Seattle's Parkway central office;
              volunteers have adapted it for the museum.
            </p>
          </ExhibitItem>
        </r-cell>
        <r-cell>
          <ExhibitItem
            title="Ringing and tone plant"
            headingLevel="h4"
            href="https://wiki.connections.museum/w/CMS:803C_Ringing_and_Tone_Plant"
            linkLabel="See how the ringing and tone plant was rebuilt"
          >
            <p>
              This equipment makes the ringing current and tones used by the panel
              and No. 1 Crossbar. Volunteers brought together a motor generator
              from Seattle's West office and a control board from Hartford,
              Connecticut, then wired them into a working exhibit. A speaker lets
              visitors hear the tones for themselves.
            </p>
          </ExhibitItem>
        </r-cell>
      </ExhibitGroup>

      <ExhibitGroup
        id="electronic-switching"
        title="Electronic switching"
        headingLevel="h3"
        columns={2}
      >
        <r-cell>
          <ExhibitItem title="Western Electric 3ESS" headingLevel="h4">
            <p>
              Electronic switches took over much of the work once done by rooms
              full of moving machinery. The 3ESS was built for smaller communities
              and could serve up to 4,500 lines. It's one of the working switches
              our volunteers care for here.
            </p>
          </ExhibitItem>
        </r-cell>
        <r-cell>
          <ExhibitItem title="What changed in a central office?" headingLevel="h4">
            <p>
              An electronic switch needed far less floor space than the mechanical
              systems it replaced. The change took planning and hands-on work: in
              some offices, a new building was added while the old switch kept
              serving callers. The museum's
              <a href="https://www.telcomhistory.org/seattleElectronic.html">older online exhibit</a>
              also traces the 5ESS and electronic key telephone systems.
            </p>
          </ExhibitItem>
        </r-cell>
      </ExhibitGroup>

      <ExhibitGroup
        id="outside-plant"
        title="Outside plant: the lines beyond the building"
        headingLevel="h3"
        columns={3}
      >
        <r-cell>
          <ExhibitItem title="Building terminals" headingLevel="h4">
            <p>
              A call needs a way into the network. Building terminals join the
              wiring from a home or business to the telephone company's circuits.
              The display follows these connections from roughly 1910 onward.
            </p>
          </ExhibitItem>
        </r-cell>
        <r-cell>
          <ExhibitItem title="Open wire" headingLevel="h4">
            <p>
              Poles, crossarms and insulators once held the two wires of a telephone
              circuit apart in the open air. That spacing helped prevent shorts
              before better insulation was available. Seattle had open-wire lines
              as early as 1883; later came cable, then fiber.
            </p>
          </ExhibitItem>
        </r-cell>
        <r-cell>
          <ExhibitItem title="Splices and linemen's tools" headingLevel="h4">
            <p>
              Splicing joins sections of wire or fiber so a line can keep going.
              Look for examples from paper-insulated copper cable through fiber,
              including a copper splice carrying 4,200 pairs. The poles, cable and
              tools here tell the story of the people who built and repaired the
              network outside the switchroom.
            </p>
          </ExhibitItem>
        </r-cell>
      </ExhibitGroup>

      <ContentSection
        id="telephones-and-station-equipment"
        title="Telephones & station equipment"
        headingLevel="h3"
      >
        <p>
          Switching is only part of the story. The collection also includes
          payphones, picturephones, Design Line sets, a Space Needle radio-telephone
          from the Century 21 World's Fair, and switchboards. Ask a guide about the
          phones you remember, or one you've never seen before.
        </p>
      </ContentSection>

      <ContentSection
        id="other-exhibit-categories"
        title="Telegraphs, teletypes & more"
        headingLevel="h3"
      >
        <p>
          Follow the story beyond the telephone: there are telegraphs, teletypes,
          and tools used by the people who built and repaired the network. Our
          guides can show you around. Please ask before handling an exhibit; some
          equipment is fragile or live.
        </p>
      </ContentSection>
    </ContentSection>
  </PageBody>
</BaseLayout>
